CH = dict(num=1, tag="Economics", theme="Scarcity, opportunity cost, PPC, three key questions, economic systems",
 title="Building Blocks in Economics: The Problem of Choice",
 intro="Money, time, land and even steel are limited, but what people want has no end. This chapter shows how that gap forces every household, firm and government to choose, what each choice costs, and how different economic systems decide the answers.",
 big_questions=["What does economics deal with?", "What are the key questions in economics?", "How do different economic systems address these questions?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "Everyday Choices, Needs and Wants"),
 ("p", "Spending pocket money on snacks or saving for shoes, deciding who gets the one library copy of a popular book, a farmer picking a crop, a firm choosing between more workers or more machines, a government choosing between highways and hospitals — all are **economic choices** made by individuals, enterprises and governments."),
 ("table", ["", "Needs", "Wants"], [["Meaning", "Essentials without which life or work is difficult", "Things we would like to have for comfort or pleasure"], ["Examples", "Food, water, shelter, medicines", "Gadgets, holidays, luxury items"], ["Nature", "Limited and fairly stable", "**Unlimited** and keep changing (bicycle → motorbike → car)"]]),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Market** is a place (physical or virtual, on the internet) where products and services are bought and sold."),
 ("h2", "Choices and Limited Resources"),
 ("ul", ["**Resources** are the factors used to produce goods and services. They may be natural (water, coal) or human-made (capital, technology).", "Factors of production, learnt in Grade 8, are **land, labour, capital and technology**.", "Both natural and human-made resources are **limited** in quantity, yet each has **many alternative uses**. Steel, for example, can go into aircraft or into refrigerators.", "Because wants are unlimited and resources limited, there is **scarcity**, and scarcity forces **choice**: households and whole economies must use scarce resources in the best possible way to improve quality of life."]),
 ("h3", "Opportunity cost"),
 ("p", "When one alternative is chosen, the others are given up. The **value of what is given up** is the **opportunity cost** of the choice."),
 ("h3", "Worked example — the farmer's barley and wheat"),
 ("p", "A farmer has one plot with limited land, water and labour and can grow barley or wheat. The possible combinations are:"),
 ("table", ["Combination", "Barley (kg)", "Wheat (kg)"], [["A", "0", "100"], ["B", "25", "90"], ["C", "50", "70"], ["D", "75", "40"], ["E", "100", "0"]]),
 ("ul", ["Plotting barley on the x-axis and wheat on the y-axis gives a **downward-sloping curve**, the **Production Possibility Curve (PPC)**.", "The PPC shows the **different combinations of goods that can be produced using all available resources efficiently**, with no wastage.", "Moving from A to E gives more barley but less wheat. The wheat sacrificed is the **opportunity cost of growing more barley**.", "Every point on the curve is a maximum output, so the PPC helps enterprises and governments plan and decide."]),
 ("note", "REMEMBER", "Opportunity cost can be read off the table: from A to B, 25 kg barley costs 10 kg wheat; from B to C, 25 kg more barley costs 20 kg wheat; from C to D it costs 30 kg wheat, and from D to E it costs 40 kg wheat. Giving up more and more wheat for each extra 25 kg of barley shows the trade-off clearly."),
 ("p", "Time is also a scarce resource: how you spend an evening is a choice with an opportunity cost."),
 ("h2", "What Does Economics Deal With?"),
 ("ul", ["The word **economics** comes from the Greek *oikonomia*, made of *oikos* (household) and *nemein* (management). Economics therefore began as **household management**; the same idea applies to nations that must plan limited resources.", "Economics studies **how choices are made to use limited resources to satisfy needs and wants**.", "It explains how **economic entities** (consumers, producers, government, enterprises, financial institutions) interact — how people work and earn wages, how wealth and resources are shared, how prices are set in the market, how education and technology drive investment, and how government policy and trade affect prices and jobs."]),
 ("p", "Good decisions need **data and analysis, not guesswork**. Families divide income among essential items (food, medicines, school supplies), non-essential items (jewellery, entertainment, restaurants) and savings. Governments use tax revenue to plan spending on infrastructure and welfare. Enterprises study market trends and new innovations to serve customers and increase profit. Economists weigh the alternatives, their opportunity costs and likely outcomes, using government reports such as economic surveys and company financial statements."),
 ("table", ["Area where economists work", "What they do"], [["Policy-making", "Guide governments on taxation and welfare spending"], ["Business consulting", "Help firms plan growth and improve efficiency"], ["Finance", "Advise investors on where to invest"], ["Research and education", "Study economic trends and teach others"]]),
 ("note", "NOTE", "The **Economic Survey of India** is an annual document of the Ministry of Finance, presented in Parliament **before the Union Budget**. It reviews the previous year's performance of agriculture, industry, services, employment, inflation, education, health and infrastructure, discusses future challenges, and works as a blueprint for the Budget. Terms to know: *survey* (systematic collection and analysis of data), *data* (facts and statistics) and *policy* (a course of action adopted by an organisation or individual)."),
 ("h2", "Key Questions in Economics"),
 ("p", "The mismatch between **unlimited wants and limited resources** creates scarcity and choices, which are summed up in three questions: **what to produce, how to produce and for whom to produce**."),
 ("h3", "What to produce (and in what quantity)"),
 ("ul", ["The economy must decide which goods and services, and how much of each, to produce over a period.", "Example: water-intensive sugarcane and paddy versus drought-resistant millets and pulses. Sugarcane gives high profit and supports the sugar industry; millets and pulses save water, improve soil health and promote sustainable farming. The opportunity cost of sugarcane is the saving in water and soil health forgone.", "This is the trade-off between **short-term gain and long-term sustainability**; companies, governments and consumers face similar trade-offs."]),
 ("h3", "For whom to produce"),
 ("ul", ["This asks who will use the goods, and how they will be produced and distributed. People differ in needs, income, taste and lifestyle, so producers choose which group to serve.", "Producers study what consumers like, how much they can pay and how much demand exists, so resources are not wasted."]),
 ("table", ["Type of shoe", "For whom", "Features"], [["School shoes", "Students", "Simple, durable, affordable"], ["Office-wear shoes", "Working professionals", "Comfort, formal look, often leather or polished materials"], ["Sports shoes", "Athletes and fitness users", "Special rubber soles, light materials for grip and support"], ["Casual shoes or slippers", "Daily users", "Comfortable yet affordable"]]),
 ("p", "The target group also affects materials: leather is aimed at office-goers and higher-income buyers, rubber or synthetic at sportspersons, factory workers and people who need affordable, durable footwear."),
 ("h3", "How to produce"),
 ("ul", ["After deciding what to produce, producers must choose methods, resources and technology, and the right mix of land, labour, capital and technology.", "**Labour-intensive** production uses more workers and less machinery (agriculture, handicrafts). **Capital-intensive** production uses more machines and technology and fewer workers (steel, automobiles)."]),
 ("table", ["Factor that influences the choice", "How it matters (garment example)"], [["Cost of capital", "If machines are costly, the firm uses more labour; if they become cheap, it may automate"], ["Level of technology", "Advanced technology encourages machines; limited technology means manual work"], ["Nature of the product", "Designer or customised clothes need skilled labour; mass-produced garments suit machines"], ["Cost and availability of labour", "Cheap, plentiful labour favours labour-intensive methods; costly or scarce labour favours machines"], ["Laws and regulations", "Labour laws or incentives for machinery shape the decision"]]),
 ("h2", "Economic Systems and How Choices Are Made"),
 ("p", "The answers to the three questions depend on how resources are organised and **who controls the decisions**. The **economic system** is the arrangement that defines how goods, services and resources are produced, consumed and distributed in a country. There are three kinds."),
 ("table", ["Feature", "Planned economy", "Market economy", "Mixed economy"], [
  ["Who decides", "A **central planning authority** of the government (for example a planning commission)", "**Demand and supply** forces with little government interference", "Private individuals, enterprises **and** the government together"],
  ["Ownership", "Government owns most land, factories, banks and transport", "Mostly **private** individuals and companies own resources", "Private ownership plus a large public sector"],
  ["Prices and output", "Fixed by government; firms follow central targets, not demand", "Prices and output set by market forces; government does not control them", "Private players are regulated by government; some goods are state-supplied"],
  ["Competition", "Strict permits and licences restrict private firms; little competition", "Many producers offer similar goods, so quality, lower prices and innovation are encouraged", "Private and public firms coexist and compete"],
  ["Weakness or role of government", "Little motive to improve quality or innovate", "Government acts like a referee: law and order, safety, **public goods** and physical infrastructure", "Fair competition rules, consumer protection, transparency, public goods, welfare programmes"],
  ["Examples", "Former Soviet Union, North Korea, Cuba", "USA, Japan, Hong Kong", "India (after 1991), China (after 1978), Germany, Sweden"]]),
 ("note", "DEFINITION", "**Public goods** are goods and services available to all without anyone being excluded, and one person's use does not prevent others from using them — for example parks, roads, police services, street lights and basic education."),
 ("p", "Pure systems rarely exist. Even market economies such as the USA and Singapore have major government involvement, so **almost all economies are mixed**. The government's role in the market can be compared to a referee in a football match."),
 ("h3", "India's economic journey"),
 ("ul", ["After Independence India followed a **state-led approach**, close to a planned economy: the government controlled industries, allocated resources and regulated production through licences and permits, and banking, transport and heavy industries were dominated by the public sector.", "By **1991** the country faced serious economic difficulties, so major reforms were introduced: less regulation, encouragement to private enterprise, opening to global trade and investment, and more competition.", "India therefore moved towards a more market-oriented system while keeping an important role for the government (a mixed economy)."]),
 ("h2", "Quick Revision: Likely Exam Questions"),
 ("qa", [
  ("Differentiate between needs and wants.", "Needs are essentials such as food, water and shelter; wants are things we desire, such as gadgets or holidays. Wants are unlimited and keep changing."),
  ("What is opportunity cost? Give an example.", "The value of the next best alternative given up when a choice is made. Growing more barley means giving up wheat, as in the farmer's table."),
  ("What does a PPC show?", "The different combinations of two goods that can be produced with all available resources used efficiently; its downward slope shows the trade-off."),
  ("Why does scarcity arise?", "Because wants are unlimited while resources such as land, labour, capital and money are limited and have alternative uses."),
  ("What is the meaning of the word 'economics'?", "From the Greek *oikonomia*: *oikos* (household) and *nemein* (management), i.e. household management."),
  ("List the three key questions of economics.", "What to produce, how to produce, and for whom to produce."),
  ("Distinguish between labour-intensive and capital-intensive production.", "Labour-intensive uses more workers and less machinery (handicrafts, agriculture); capital-intensive uses more machines and fewer workers (steel, cars)."),
  ("Which factors decide the choice of production technique?", "Cost of capital and labour, level of technology, nature of product, and laws and regulations."),
  ("Compare planned and market economies.", "Planned: government decides, owns resources, limited competition, little innovation. Market: demand and supply decide, private ownership, competition leads to quality and innovation."),
  ("Why are most economies called mixed economies?", "They combine private enterprise and market forces with government regulation, public goods and welfare; pure systems rarely exist."),
  ("What are public goods?", "Goods and services available to everyone without exclusion, such as roads, parks and street lights."),
  ("Why was 1991 important for India?", "A serious economic crisis led to reforms that reduced regulation, encouraged private firms, opened trade and increased competition."),
 ]),
 ],
 takeaways=["Economics is about **choices** made with **limited resources** to satisfy **unlimited wants**.", "Every choice has an **opportunity cost**; the **PPC** shows the trade-off between two goods.", "Three key questions: **what, how and for whom** to produce.", "**Planned** economies rely on government control, **market** economies on demand and supply, **mixed** economies on both.", "Almost every economy today, including **India after 1991**, is a mixed economy.", "Good economic decisions rest on **data and analysis**."])
